1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
And now it's the old Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:12,800
Yesterday I published something I want you to go look at over at ScoutmasterCGcom.

3
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:17,720
Look for the pioneering tower challenge. okay,

4
00:00:17,720 --> 00:00:23,600
The challenge is to build the tower that you see in the sketch there

5
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:26,360
And send me a picture of the result.

6
00:00:26,360 --> 00:00:28,360
It won't be all that easy.

7
00:00:28,360 --> 00:00:30,360
It may not even be possible.

8
00:00:30,360 --> 00:00:32,360
Take a look at it,

9
00:00:32,360 --> 00:00:34,360
Let me know what you think.

10
00:00:34,360 --> 00:00:36,360
Hey, this is podcast number 190..

11
00:00:56,360 --> 00:01:06,360
Well, welcome back to the Scoutmaster podcast.

12
00:01:06,360 --> 00:01:08,360
This is Clark Green.

13
00:01:08,360 --> 00:01:10,360
Let's take a look at the mailbag.

14
00:01:10,360 --> 00:01:18,360
We heard from Damien Law, who is the Cubmaster of PAC-86, an assistant scoutmaster with Troop-86 in Tenefly, New Jersey.

15
00:01:18,360 --> 00:01:23,360
Damien said this: I was intrigued by the situation mentioned in your last podcast

16
00:01:23,360 --> 00:01:29,360
where the Patrol Leaders Council decided to organize the patrols to its liking without asking the scouts.

17
00:01:29,360 --> 00:01:32,360
It wasn't clear to me what you would do if their scheme works out.

18
00:01:32,360 --> 00:01:36,360
Do you just let it stand, or do you overrule them and crush their leadership?

19
00:01:36,360 --> 00:01:41,360
What do you do if you have a patrol which decided that it won't accept any more members,

20
00:01:41,360 --> 00:01:44,360
Yet you've got other scouts who want to join that patrol?

21
00:01:44,360 --> 00:01:48,360
What do you do if you have a special needs scout who needs supervision

22
00:01:48,360 --> 00:01:51,360
and is only half-heartedly wanted by any of the patrols.

23
00:01:51,360 --> 00:01:53,360
Damien, thanks for getting in touch.

24
00:01:53,360 --> 00:01:54,360
I'm glad you're listening.

25
00:01:54,360 --> 00:01:56,360
I'm glad you took the time to get in touch and ask those questions.

26
00:01:56,360 --> 00:01:58,360
So let me give you an answer.

27
00:01:58,360 --> 00:02:05,360
If the choices that the Patrol Leaders Council made work, there's not going to be any reason to overrule them.

28
00:02:05,360 --> 00:02:09,360
My advice in that situation is to observe and see what happens

29
00:02:09,360 --> 00:02:14,360
and then talk to the Senior Patrol Leader and encourage him to make changes. if things don't work,

30
00:02:14,360 --> 00:02:16,360
You know, sometimes there's a course correction needed,

31
00:02:16,360 --> 00:02:20,360
but there's never any call to crush their leadership. like you say,

32
00:02:20,360 --> 00:02:26,360
Youth leadership in the patrol system is working with scouts to follow the program and get things right.

33
00:02:26,360 --> 00:02:31,360
It's not just throwing a switch and saying it was the boys' decision so I really can't do anything about it.

34
00:02:31,360 --> 00:02:39,360
We collaborate with them and we help them find ways to solve problems and to deal with tough situations.

35
00:02:39,360 --> 00:02:43,360
You know, in one way of looking at it, my scouts do the wrong thing all the time,

36
00:02:43,360 --> 00:02:45,360
at least what I consider wrong,

37
00:02:45,360 --> 00:02:51,360
Unless it's something that's just totally inappropriate or dangerous, I let them go ahead and try it out.

38
00:02:51,360 --> 00:02:57,360
They seem to learn pretty quickly if they have something wrong, because it just doesn't work very well.

39
00:02:57,360 --> 00:03:03,360
The answer to the other two questions that you have there is that scouting is never exclusive,

40
00:03:03,360 --> 00:03:05,360
It's inclusive.

41
00:03:05,360 --> 00:03:09,360
A patrol close to members is something I've never had happen.

42
00:03:09,360 --> 00:03:14,360
If a patrol thought this way, I'd ask them what part of the scout oath and law they had based their decision on.

43
00:03:15,360 --> 00:03:20,360
You know, is it that a scout is friendly and kind or to help other people at all times?

44
00:03:20,360 --> 00:03:22,360
It just doesn't jive, does it,

45
00:03:22,360 --> 00:03:28,360
And bringing that to their attention is going to get them to discover that maybe that's really not going to work.

46
00:03:28,360 --> 00:03:36,360
If there's a scout who's not real popular and who nobody likes- and I've had that happen on a number of occasions-

47
00:03:36,360 --> 00:03:43,360
I will sit down with the PLC and ask them to empathize, to think about what it feels like if nobody likes you.

48
00:03:43,360 --> 00:03:47,360
I ask them if they want to have a troop just for the people they like

49
00:03:47,360 --> 00:03:51,360
or if they want to have a troop for anyone who's willing to be a scout.

50
00:03:51,360 --> 00:03:57,360
Every time we have this situation and we have a little talk like this, they get it and they make changes

51
00:03:57,360 --> 00:03:59,360
and they go out of their way to make amends.

52
00:03:59,360 --> 00:04:05,360
I've had plenty of scouts with special needs and none of them have really needed adult supervision

53
00:04:05,360 --> 00:04:09,360
And none of them have needed an adult right next to them all the time.

54
00:04:09,360 --> 00:04:13,360
Now there are parents and scouters who thought that we did need to do that,

55
00:04:13,360 --> 00:04:15,360
but time proved that we really didn't.

56
00:04:15,360 --> 00:04:23,360
A lot of the difficulties that come with special needs are not quite so difficult. when scouts are friendly and kind to each other,

57
00:04:23,360 --> 00:04:27,360
It doesn't take a whole lot of encouragement to make that happen, in my experience.

58
00:04:27,360 --> 00:04:29,360
Thanks for getting in touch once again, Damon,

59
00:04:29,360 --> 00:04:34,360
And remember this is all about having a conversation, so you need to get in touch too,

60
00:04:34,360 --> 00:04:40,360
And you can do that by emailing me at clark at scoutmastercgcom.

61
00:04:40,360 --> 00:04:46,360
Hey, I want to point out again that I've got a new podcast archive over at scoutmastercgcom.

62
00:04:46,360 --> 00:04:52,360
If you get on the site, you'll see a link to the new podcast archive right there in the main menu.

63
00:04:52,360 --> 00:05:00,360
You can listen to the backlog of podcasts and download them in a way that I hope is a lot easier than it used to be.

64
00:05:00,360 --> 00:05:05,360
This coming Sunday, October 13th, is our next scout circle and it's going to happen

65
00:05:05,360 --> 00:05:11,360
Once again. our guest is going to be Joe Jacaruso from gossamergearcom.

66
00:05:11,360 --> 00:05:17,360
Joe is an experienced scouter and backpacker and he's led scouts on many miles of backpacking trips,

67
00:05:17,360 --> 00:05:21,360
And we're going to be talking specifically about scouts and backpacking.

68
00:05:21,360 --> 00:05:25,360
Scout Circle is a live presentation. It lasts about an hour.

69
00:05:25,360 --> 00:05:31,360
We begin with our guest and an interview and then we open it up to questions of people who are watching on the web.

70
00:05:31,360 --> 00:05:39,360
So go to scoutcirclecom Sunday, October 13th, from 9 to 10 pm Eastern Standard Time,

71
00:05:39,360 --> 00:05:44,360
and you can join us and Joe Jacaruso and talk about backpacking and scouts.

72
00:05:44,360 --> 00:05:50,360
And before we get going today, I have a pre-announcement announcement of a pending thing that will happen

73
00:05:50,360 --> 00:05:55,360
sooner or later in the not too distant future.

74
00:05:55,360 --> 00:06:00,360
What I'm talking about is the publication of my new book, The Scouting Journey,

75
00:06:00,360 --> 00:06:05,360
which is a scouter's guide to navigating the challenge, adventure and achievement of scouting.

76
00:06:05,360 --> 00:06:09,360
If all goes right, the book's going to be published in mid-November.

77
00:06:09,360 --> 00:06:11,360
Let me tell you a little bit about it.

78
00:06:11,360 --> 00:06:17,360
About a century ago, Baden Powell published a booklet that really kind of laid out all the basics of scouting,

79
00:06:17,360 --> 00:06:20,360
called AIDS to Scout Mastership.

80
00:06:20,360 --> 00:06:25,360
It's still a really valuable thing to read and any serious scouter would want to do it.

81
00:06:25,360 --> 00:06:30,360
But Baden Powell was writing a century ago and his turns of phrase and his allegories have not aged

82
00:06:30,360 --> 00:06:33,360
as well as a lot of his ideas have.

83
00:06:33,360 --> 00:06:40,360
So, taking that work as my guide, I have attempted to write a book to the present generation of scouters

84
00:06:40,360 --> 00:06:47,360
that applies my understanding of what Baden Powell wrote in a way that's relevant a century later,

85
00:06:47,360 --> 00:06:54,360
As with AIDS, to Scout Mastership- The Scouting Journey. it's not going to be like a step-by-step how-to guide

86
00:06:54,360 --> 00:06:58,360
but a study of the broad underlying concepts of scouting.

87
00:06:58,360 --> 00:07:00,360
I tried to keep it jargon-free.

88
00:07:00,360 --> 00:07:05,360
You've no doubt seen the quizzical look on people's faces when you start talking about the SM

89
00:07:05,360 --> 00:07:09,360
and CC working together with the PLC or talking to the SPL.

90
00:07:09,360 --> 00:07:16,360
I've consciously avoided using organizational scouting terms in an attempt to present those broad underlying concepts

91
00:07:16,360 --> 00:07:19,360
as clearly and simply as possible.

92
00:07:19,360 --> 00:07:25,360
You know after all these years of talking to people and study and practical experience and reading,

93
00:07:25,360 --> 00:07:32,360
it's going to be impossible to claim that anything in that book is exclusively my own idea

94
00:07:32,360 --> 00:07:35,360
And I doubt much of it is going to be all that original.

95
00:07:35,360 --> 00:07:42,360
What it is is my sincere attempt to explain scouting and share some of my experiences in putting it into practice.

96
00:07:42,360 --> 00:07:48,360
You'll be the judge if I succeeded in that or not, but hopefully it will be something that will help new scouters

97
00:07:48,360 --> 00:07:54,360
find their footing and maybe inspire some of us veterans to see familiar things with a fresh set of eyes.

98
00:07:54,360 --> 00:07:59,360
So The Scouting Journey. it's due to be published in mid-November.

99
00:07:59,360 --> 00:08:04,360
We'll be talking about it more and you'll see it on the website as things progress

100
00:08:04,360 --> 00:08:09,360
Well, in this edition of the Scoutmaster podcast and Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less,

101
00:08:09,360 --> 00:08:15,360
we're going to talk about a recent post on the blog about 15-minute patrol leader training

102
00:08:15,360 --> 00:08:22,360
And then I've got a little Scoutmasters minute for you that asks the question: do you have what it takes?

103
00:08:22,360 --> 00:08:25,360
All of that is going to take up the rest of the podcast.

104
00:08:25,360 --> 00:08:27,360
So let's get started, shall we?

105
00:08:27,360 --> 00:08:32,360
Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

106
00:08:35,360 --> 00:08:44,360
So I found it useful to think of patrol leader training as an ongoing process of coaching and mentoring.

107
00:08:44,360 --> 00:08:48,360
It's not just a single event, just something that you can get done in a weekend.

108
00:08:48,360 --> 00:08:54,360
Good coaching comes from a solid, simple, shared understanding of the fundamentals.

109
00:08:54,360 --> 00:08:59,360
Now, to my mind, a trained patrol leader knows those fundamental things

110
00:08:59,360 --> 00:09:05,360
and I can get the fundamentals across to a patrol leader in about 15 minutes.

111
00:09:05,360 --> 00:09:09,360
I usually do this just before or just after a patrol leader's council meeting

112
00:09:09,360 --> 00:09:15,360
and I repeat pieces of what I'm about to tell you on a regular basis.

113
00:09:15,360 --> 00:09:20,360
Now, the kind of training that I'm talking about is based in asking Scouts questions

114
00:09:20,360 --> 00:09:25,360
and having set specific answers for each of those questions.

115
00:09:25,360 --> 00:09:27,360
It's kind of like a catechism.

116
00:09:27,360 --> 00:09:32,360
I've used this training method for a few years and my older Scouts know all the answers

117
00:09:32,360 --> 00:09:34,360
and they're usually able to give them verbatim.

118
00:09:34,360 --> 00:09:37,360
So they join right in and they help me when I'm doing this.

119
00:09:37,360 --> 00:09:39,360
Does it just take 15 minutes?

120
00:09:39,360 --> 00:09:42,360
Yes, it just takes 15 minutes.

121
00:09:42,360 --> 00:09:46,360
I work really hard to avoid any kind of commentary and digression.

122
00:09:46,360 --> 00:09:49,360
I stick to the main ideas and I keep things moving

123
00:09:49,360 --> 00:09:53,360
And then I finish up by repeating each question in sequence

124
00:09:53,360 --> 00:09:56,360
and try and cement the answers in the minds of my patrol leaders.

125
00:09:56,360 --> 00:10:01,360
If you go to ScoutMasterCGcom and you look for 15-minute patrol leader training,

126
00:10:01,360 --> 00:10:05,360
you'll find a PDF file that outlines this whole thing

127
00:10:05,360 --> 00:10:08,360
And you may want to give that to your patrol leaders,

128
00:10:08,360 --> 00:10:10,360
but don't give them to it until after you're done,

129
00:10:10,360 --> 00:10:14,360
Otherwise they're just going to be looking at a piece of paper and reading ahead.

130
00:10:14,360 --> 00:10:17,360
You want them engaged and in the moment to make this work

131
00:10:17,360 --> 00:10:22,360
And, lastly, don't announce that you're going to train them.

132
00:10:22,360 --> 00:10:24,360
Just have a conversation with your Scouts.

133
00:10:24,360 --> 00:10:26,360
So here's how this goes:

134
00:10:26,360 --> 00:10:29,360
Either just before or just after the senior patrol leader

135
00:10:29,360 --> 00:10:32,360
is finished with his patrol leaders council meeting,

136
00:10:32,360 --> 00:10:35,360
I want to ask the patrol leaders some questions

137
00:10:35,360 --> 00:10:38,360
And I'll turn to them and I'll say: what's your goal?

138
00:10:38,360 --> 00:10:42,360
Now they may stare at me for a little while and try and figure out what I'm asking.

139
00:10:42,360 --> 00:10:45,360
They may try and figure out a right answer

140
00:10:45,360 --> 00:10:49,360
And I'll look for elements of the answer I'm looking for in their answers.

141
00:10:49,360 --> 00:10:53,360
But the set answer to that question is to lead, train

142
00:10:53,360 --> 00:10:56,360
and inspire Scouts to become first class.

143
00:10:56,360 --> 00:10:58,360
So what is your goal as a patrol leader?

144
00:10:58,360 --> 00:11:02,360
To lead, train and inspire Scouts to become first class,

145
00:11:02,360 --> 00:11:07,360
Because we know when Scouts reach first class, that we're keeping our promises to them.

146
00:11:07,360 --> 00:11:10,360
We know that they're going camping and working in their patrol

147
00:11:10,360 --> 00:11:12,360
and learning the skills of scouting.

148
00:11:12,360 --> 00:11:14,360
So let me ask you this question:

149
00:11:14,360 --> 00:11:16,360
How do Scouts become first class?

150
00:11:16,360 --> 00:11:18,360
Well, they'll come up with all kinds of answers

151
00:11:18,360 --> 00:11:23,360
They'll come up with. well, they do requirements and things like that.

152
00:11:23,360 --> 00:11:27,360
And the answer I'm aiming at, and the answer that I'll supply for them,

153
00:11:27,360 --> 00:11:30,360
is by doing things that Scouts do.

154
00:11:30,360 --> 00:11:32,360
So how do Scouts become first class?

155
00:11:32,360 --> 00:11:35,360
By doing things that Scouts do.

156
00:11:35,360 --> 00:11:37,360
It's a little difficult to understand the difference

157
00:11:37,360 --> 00:11:41,360
between completing requirements and doing things that Scouts do.

158
00:11:41,360 --> 00:11:45,360
If we plan and prepare for doing things that Scouts do,

159
00:11:45,360 --> 00:11:50,360
they will complete the requirements as a result of participating.

160
00:11:50,360 --> 00:11:53,360
If we plan to do requirements,

161
00:11:53,360 --> 00:11:56,360
we often create kind of a classroom experience

162
00:11:56,360 --> 00:11:59,360
where you go over material because you have to

163
00:11:59,360 --> 00:12:02,360
rather than you're doing something because you want to.

164
00:12:02,360 --> 00:12:07,360
So I tell my patrol leaders: we don't quote, do requirements, unquote.

165
00:12:07,360 --> 00:12:12,360
We complete requirements because we do the things that Scouts do.

166
00:12:12,360 --> 00:12:14,360
It sounds a little confusing at first,

167
00:12:14,360 --> 00:12:17,360
but it begins to make more sense as you think about it.

168
00:12:17,360 --> 00:12:21,360
So my next question to them is: what do Scouts do?

169
00:12:21,360 --> 00:12:23,360
And they'll have all kinds of answers for this

170
00:12:23,360 --> 00:12:26,360
and I'll find elements of the set answer in them.

171
00:12:26,360 --> 00:12:28,360
Then I'll share what the answer is.

172
00:12:28,360 --> 00:12:30,360
Scouts go camping,

173
00:12:30,360 --> 00:12:32,360
They learn skills and apply them to their activities,

174
00:12:32,360 --> 00:12:34,360
They explore hobbies and careers,

175
00:12:34,360 --> 00:12:36,360
They serve their community,

176
00:12:36,360 --> 00:12:39,360
They're responsible to organize and plan their own activities

177
00:12:39,360 --> 00:12:42,360
and they put the Scout oath and law into practice.

178
00:12:42,360 --> 00:12:44,360
Now, that's kind of a long answer

179
00:12:44,360 --> 00:12:47,360
and we never really get it absolutely 100%

180
00:12:47,360 --> 00:12:49,360
the same way every time,

181
00:12:49,360 --> 00:12:52,360
but it's the spirit of that answer that I'm looking for.

182
00:12:52,360 --> 00:12:55,360
Almost all of what Scouts do happens in a patrol,

183
00:12:55,360 --> 00:12:58,360
so the patrol leader's job is really important.

184
00:12:58,360 --> 00:13:00,360
Everything the patrol leader does

185
00:13:00,360 --> 00:13:03,360
is centered on serving Scouts and his patrol,

186
00:13:03,360 --> 00:13:06,360
making it possible for them to become first class

187
00:13:06,360 --> 00:13:08,360
by doing the things that Scouts do.

188
00:13:08,360 --> 00:13:11,360
He's entrusted with fulfilling all the promises

189
00:13:11,360 --> 00:13:13,360
of Scouting for his patrol.

190
00:13:13,360 --> 00:13:17,360
So so far, we know that your job is to lead, train

191
00:13:17,360 --> 00:13:19,360
and inspire Scouts to become first class.

192
00:13:19,360 --> 00:13:21,360
We know that Scouts become first class

193
00:13:21,360 --> 00:13:23,360
by doing the things that Scouts do.

194
00:13:23,360 --> 00:13:26,360
So the next question is: how do Scouts do these things?

195
00:13:26,360 --> 00:13:29,360
Well, they do them by attending

196
00:13:29,360 --> 00:13:31,360
and being prepared to participate

197
00:13:31,360 --> 00:13:33,360
in the events that you plan.

198
00:13:33,360 --> 00:13:35,360
It's really simple, isn't it?

199
00:13:35,360 --> 00:13:38,360
But it's something that we always need to be thinking about.

200
00:13:38,360 --> 00:13:40,360
If a Scout isn't participating,

201
00:13:40,360 --> 00:13:42,360
we have to ask ourselves: why?

202
00:13:42,360 --> 00:13:44,360
Is he busy with other things?

203
00:13:44,360 --> 00:13:46,360
Is our program interesting and engaging?

204
00:13:46,360 --> 00:13:48,360
Does he want to be here?

205
00:13:48,360 --> 00:13:50,360
Did his patrol leader talk to him?

206
00:13:50,360 --> 00:13:52,360
Your example and your encouragement

207
00:13:52,360 --> 00:13:55,360
is the most important factor

208
00:13:55,360 --> 00:13:58,360
in getting Scouts to participate.

209
00:13:58,360 --> 00:14:00,360
So for them to participate, they need to know

210
00:14:00,360 --> 00:14:02,360
what's going on.

211
00:14:02,360 --> 00:14:04,360
So my next question is: how do Scouts know

212
00:14:04,360 --> 00:14:06,360
about what we're doing?

213
00:14:06,360 --> 00:14:08,360
Well, patrol leaders communicate

214
00:14:08,360 --> 00:14:10,360
in details a Scout needs to know

215
00:14:10,360 --> 00:14:12,360
and the preparations a Scout needs to make.

216
00:14:12,360 --> 00:14:14,360
Now, at this point in my

217
00:14:14,360 --> 00:14:16,360
little 15 minute training,

218
00:14:16,360 --> 00:14:18,360
I will explain that communication

219
00:14:18,360 --> 00:14:20,360
only happens when we close the loop,

220
00:14:20,360 --> 00:14:22,360
And closing the loop means

221
00:14:22,360 --> 00:14:24,360
you're actually speaking to a person

222
00:14:24,360 --> 00:14:26,360
and you get a reply to what you've said.

223
00:14:26,360 --> 00:14:28,360
So leaving a phone message

224
00:14:28,360 --> 00:14:30,360
on the answering machine or a text message

225
00:14:30,360 --> 00:14:32,360
or an email really doesn't count

226
00:14:32,360 --> 00:14:34,360
as communication.

227
00:14:34,360 --> 00:14:36,360
The only thing that counts as communication

228
00:14:36,360 --> 00:14:38,360
is to close the loop.

229
00:14:38,360 --> 00:14:40,360
Now I may demonstrate this by

230
00:14:40,360 --> 00:14:42,360
imitating a phone call from the patrol

231
00:14:42,360 --> 00:14:44,360
leader to a Scout in his patrol

232
00:14:44,360 --> 00:14:46,360
and showing them how to close the loop

233
00:14:46,360 --> 00:14:48,360
by asking the Scout to tell me

