00:20:14,360 --> 00:20:16,360
it works pretty well.

398
00:20:16,360 --> 00:20:18,360
it takes about 15 minutes.

399
00:20:18,360 --> 00:20:20,360
then, during the time that I'll take

400
00:20:20,360 --> 00:20:22,360
before or after a patrol leader's council meeting

401
00:20:22,360 --> 00:20:24,360
when an issue comes up,

402
00:20:24,360 --> 00:20:26,360
we'll return to one of those questions

403
00:20:26,360 --> 00:20:28,360
and we'll examine them a little bit more

404
00:20:28,360 --> 00:20:30,360
and we'll say, okay, well,

405
00:20:30,360 --> 00:20:32,360
we're kind of missing the answer to this one,

406
00:20:32,360 --> 00:20:34,360
we're not doing what we know that we need to do

407
00:20:34,360 --> 00:20:36,360
on this one and it really, really helps.

408
00:20:36,360 --> 00:20:38,360
it's simple,

409
00:20:38,360 --> 00:20:40,360
it's brief and it creates an impression

410
00:20:40,360 --> 00:20:42,360
in their minds that they find actually

411
00:20:42,360 --> 00:20:44,360
helpful in putting things together

412
00:20:44,360 --> 00:20:46,360
and in doing their job as a patrol

413
00:20:46,360 --> 00:20:48,360
leader's council.

414
00:21:10,360 --> 00:21:12,360
Do you have what it takes?

415
00:21:12,360 --> 00:21:14,360
I mean, do you have what it takes

416
00:21:14,360 --> 00:21:16,360
to be a scouter?

417
00:21:16,360 --> 00:21:18,360
I think scouters are generally

418
00:21:18,360 --> 00:21:20,360
very wonderful, big hearted people

419
00:21:20,360 --> 00:21:22,360
by nature,

420
00:21:22,360 --> 00:21:24,360
so you probably already have

421
00:21:24,360 --> 00:21:26,360
plenty to work with.

422
00:21:26,360 --> 00:21:28,360
but any worthwhile journey

423
00:21:28,360 --> 00:21:30,360
you'll have to go through

424
00:21:30,360 --> 00:21:32,360
and you'll have to go through,

425
00:21:32,360 --> 00:21:34,360
and you'll have to go through

426
00:21:34,360 --> 00:21:36,360
and you'll have to go through

427
00:21:36,360 --> 00:21:38,360
and you'll have to go through

428
00:21:38,360 --> 00:21:40,360
to what I need to work with.

429
00:21:40,360 --> 00:21:42,360
but any worthwhile journey-

430
00:21:42,360 --> 00:21:44,360
and being a scouter is a worthwhile journey-

431
00:21:44,360 --> 00:21:46,360
is going to challenge our character

432
00:21:46,360 --> 00:21:48,360
and resolve.

433
00:21:48,360 --> 00:21:50,360
we learn and grow, as our Scouts do,

434
00:21:50,360 --> 00:21:52,360
by being open to the lessons

435
00:21:52,360 --> 00:21:54,360
and experiences that we encounter along the way.

436
00:21:54,360 --> 00:21:56,360
but what do you need to start with?

437
00:21:56,360 --> 00:21:58,360
well, I think you need faith in scouting.

438
00:21:58,360 --> 00:22:00,360
anyone who's followed a set of directions

439
00:22:00,360 --> 00:22:02,360
on a map or a recipe has exercised

440
00:22:02,360 --> 00:22:04,360
exactly the kind of faith

441
00:22:04,360 --> 00:22:06,360
that a scouter needs

442
00:22:06,360 --> 00:22:11,640
claims. There's a century of relevant and useful experience behind scouting that can certainly

443
00:22:11,640 --> 00:22:15,880
help us along the way. Perhaps you're like me and you have a healthy dose of skepticism and a

444
00:22:15,880 --> 00:22:21,560
strong personality and you really don't like to follow directions. Well, follow them anyway.

445
00:22:21,560 --> 00:22:27,400
See if they make sense. Scouters aren't expected to know what's around the next bend, We're just

446
00:22:27,400 --> 00:22:33,880
expected to stay on the trail. I think a scouter needs integrity. I think scouters need to be

447
00:22:33,960 --> 00:22:40,200
who they are, not some idealized concept of what you think a scouter ought to be.

448
00:22:40,200 --> 00:22:45,960
Scouts can spot a hypocrite at 100 yards. They'll respect people in spite of imperfections,

449
00:22:45,960 --> 00:22:50,280
but they really won't give the time of day to anyone pretending to be something that they're not.

450
00:22:50,280 --> 00:22:56,040
Perhaps the finest example our scouts can have in life is someone who struggles successfully

451
00:22:56,040 --> 00:22:59,880
against the same imperfections and disappointments they encounter in themselves.

452
00:23:00,440 --> 00:23:07,560
Don't sugarcoat things and don't attempt to teach lessons. Just be honest. Scouts disdain trickery and

453
00:23:07,560 --> 00:23:13,640
subterfuge in that kind of adult way of hiding medicine in a spoonful of sugar. They can sense

454
00:23:13,640 --> 00:23:19,240
that and they don't like it. Scouting is a transformational journey. It's not a set of

455
00:23:19,240 --> 00:23:24,680
unpleasant lessons in a sugarcoating of camping and play. You need to have vision.

456
00:23:24,760 --> 00:23:31,160
Greenbar Bill said a happy boy is a good boy and a good boy is a good citizen. So everything

457
00:23:31,160 --> 00:23:37,960
in scouting is designed to support the success and happiness of our scouts. Now, at first blush,

458
00:23:37,960 --> 00:23:44,200
happiness seems like a pretty broad, indistinct vision. But think about what marks a happy scout.

459
00:23:44,200 --> 00:23:49,640
It's not just placating them or steering them away from difficulty. Scouts are happy when

460
00:23:49,640 --> 00:23:54,280
they're pursuing the challenge and adventure and achievement of the journey. They're active,

461
00:23:54,280 --> 00:23:59,240
engaged and energetic, finding satisfaction and purpose in the things that they do.

462
00:23:59,240 --> 00:24:05,240
If we focus on happiness, we're revealing the genuine joy of achievement, of taking the road

463
00:24:05,240 --> 00:24:11,160
less traveled rather than the path of least resistance. We demonstrate this in our example

464
00:24:11,160 --> 00:24:16,200
of really enjoying what we're up to and our attitude of genuine excitement in meeting

465
00:24:16,200 --> 00:24:21,320
the difficulties we encounter head on and speaking words of honest encouragement.

466
00:24:21,320 --> 00:24:26,679
A scouter needs to have compassion, not just for other people but for themselves.

467
00:24:26,679 --> 00:24:32,679
Compassionate people work to help relieve the difficulties of others. They empathize with others

468
00:24:32,679 --> 00:24:38,600
and they strive to always do unto others as they would have them do unto you, And that kind of

469
00:24:38,600 --> 00:24:43,879
compassion creates its own energy. Scouters work to make other people's lives better,

470
00:24:43,879 --> 00:24:50,040
and this makes their own lives richer and happier. Scouters need patience and persistence.

471
00:24:50,040 --> 00:24:55,560
Now, I admire people who are not easily perturbed or excited or upset or frustrated or irritated.

472
00:24:55,560 --> 00:24:59,080
You know those kind of people who are calm and composed and level headed.

473
00:25:00,120 --> 00:25:05,399
Maybe, maybe, I'll be able to be one of those people someday. Until then, I'm going to do the

474
00:25:05,399 --> 00:25:10,440
same thing that you do. I have to take a few deep breaths and count to ten, or step away for a

475
00:25:10,440 --> 00:25:16,600
couple of moments to regain my control and patience and perspective. Sometimes I find myself

476
00:25:16,600 --> 00:25:22,280
concentrating on one thing that's wrong and a landscape of success. I worry about what didn't

477
00:25:22,280 --> 00:25:27,800
happen or about what's to happen so much that I miss what's happening right now. You know, you

478
00:25:27,800 --> 00:25:33,000
finally figure out that perfection is a pretty rare commodity and we need to learn to appreciate

479
00:25:33,000 --> 00:25:38,040
what's happening right now. Working with young people is invigorating, but it can be really

480
00:25:38,040 --> 00:25:43,320
exasperating at times too. Their energy and willingness to learn is sometimes equal to

481
00:25:43,320 --> 00:25:48,439
surpassed by indecisiveness and stubbornness. And if you're like me, sometimes you feel your

482
00:25:48,439 --> 00:25:53,960
paddling against the stream, but even so, you just keep on paddling. We need to be persistent.

483
00:25:53,960 --> 00:25:59,159
We need to have some tolerance for adversity and uncertainty. We're on a challenging journey

484
00:25:59,159 --> 00:26:04,840
and it's not designed to be easy or comfortable, And scouters need a little hutzpah. You know

485
00:26:04,840 --> 00:26:10,439
what hutzpah is. It's a great old Yiddish word and it describes a combination of gall and nerve

486
00:26:10,440 --> 00:26:15,720
and guts and presumption with a little tinge of arrogance. I don't want people to be arrogant,

487
00:26:15,720 --> 00:26:21,880
but scouters can use a little hutzpah. You'll need to stand up for your scouts and for yourself

488
00:26:21,880 --> 00:26:27,720
and for the ideas essential to scouting. It takes hutzpah to be a scout, to stand out and

489
00:26:27,720 --> 00:26:34,120
to achieve in a world that sometimes belittles anyone who dares to explore their own potential.

490
00:26:34,120 --> 00:26:39,320
It takes hutzpah not only to try new ideas and methods, but to stick to the ones that

491
00:26:39,320 --> 00:26:45,000
